NSF-NIH Smart Health and Biomedical Research in the Era of Artificial Intelligence and Advanced Data Science (SCH)
NSF Solicitation 25-542  ·  Integrative Track
Project Title:
CHIP-PULSE: Clonal Hematopoiesis–Integrated Purinergic and Longitudinal Signature Explainability for Immunotherapy Response Prediction in Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer
Principal Investigator:
Marina Chiara Garassino, M.D.
The University of Chicago Medicine / Argonne National Laboratory
Target Submission Date: September 10, 2026
Budget: $1,200,000 total costs over 4 years (Integrative Track)
[DRAFT — August 30, 2026 — For Internal Review]

PROJECT SUMMARY
Overview
Immunotherapy with immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICI) has transformed the treatment of advanced non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), yet fewer than 40% of patients achieve durable clinical benefit. Current biomarkers such as PD-L1 expression and tumor mutational burden fail to capture the systemic immune context in which tumor responses occur. Clonal hematopoiesis of indeterminate potential (CHIP), defined by age-related somatic mutations in hematopoietic stem cells predominantly affecting DNMT3A, TET2, and ASXL1, has emerged as a systemic modifier of immune function detected in >50% of heavy smokers who develop lung cancer. We and others have demonstrated that CHIP shapes the immunosuppressive myeloid microenvironment through aberrant purinergic signaling specifically an eATP–P2X7 receptor–extracellular vesicle (EV) feed-forward loop that promotes ICI resistance independent of established biomarkers. This project, CHIP-PULSE, will develop and validate an explainable AI (xAI) framework that integrates CHIP clonal dynamics, purinergic biomarkers, spatial multi-omics, radiomic features, and longitudinal liquid biopsy data to predict ICI response and early treatment failure in advanced NSCLC. Leveraging the I3LUNG prospective multimodal cohort (n>3,500 patients, 18 institutions, 10 countries; Nature Medicine, 2025) and the Aurora exascale computing infrastructure at Argonne National Laboratory, we will train, validate, and clinically deploy the first AI system purpose-built for CHIP-aware ICI response stratification in NSCLC.
Intellectual Merit
CHIP-PULSE advances foundational knowledge at the intersection of computational medicine and cancer immunology by (1) developing novel AI architectures for integrating heterogeneous longitudinal biomedical data across molecular, imaging, and clinical modalities; (2) establishing mechanistic interpretability methods that reveal which CHIP genotype–purinergic feature combinations drive model predictions; and (3) creating validated computational tools for dynamic, real-time treatment response monitoring. The cross-disciplinary integration of exascale AI computing (Argonne National Laboratory) with prospective international clinical oncology data (UChicago I3LUNG) represents a genuinely novel approach that neither discipline could achieve independently.
Broader Impacts
CHIP is present in 10–15% of individuals over age 70 and >50% of heavy smokers who develop lung cancer — a population that encompasses a substantial fraction of all NSCLC patients in the United States and globally. A validated CHIP-aware AI risk stratification tool will directly improve clinical decision-making for hundreds of the thousands of patients annually, reducing unnecessary ICI toxicity, and enabling biomarker-selected clinical trial enrollment. All models, training datasets (de-identified), and analysis pipelines will be released as open-source, interactive resources. Graduate and postdoctoral training will span computational oncology, biomedical AI, and translational medicine, advancing the next generation of cross-disciplinary scientists.
 
PROJECT DESCRIPTION
1. Introduction and Significance
1.1 The Unresolved Biomarker Problem in NSCLC Immunotherapy
Lung cancer remains the leading cause of cancer mortality worldwide, accounting for approximately 1.8 million deaths annually. The introduction of anti-PD-1/PD-L1 immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICI) has produced durable remissions in a subset of patients with advanced NSCLC, but the majority do not benefit, and predictive biomarkers remain inadequate. PD-L1 immunohistochemistry and tumor mutational burden (TMB), the only FDA-approved predictive markers, have limited sensitivity and specificity and fail to capture the systemic immune state in which tumor–immune interactions occur. The central unresolved problem is that ICI response is determined not only by intrinsic tumor properties, but by the patient's systemic immune architecture — particularly myeloid cell composition and function — which existing biomarker strategies ignore entirely.
1.2 CHIP as a Systemic Immune Modifier in Lung Cancer
Clonal hematopoiesis of indeterminate potential (CHIP) arises when hematopoietic stem cells acquire somatic driver mutations — most commonly in DNMT3A (R882H), TET2, and ASXL1 — and expand clonally in the absence of overt hematologic malignancy. CHIP prevalence increases dramatically with age and carcinogen exposure: it is detected in 10–15% of adults over 65, rising to >50% of heavy smokers who develop lung cancer. Critically, CHIP-mutant myeloid progeny exhibits pathological inflammatory programming: they overproduce IL-1β, IL-6, and TNF-α; accumulate extracellular ATP (eATP); upregulate P2X7 receptor signaling; and release immunosuppressive P2X7-positive extracellular vesicles (EVs) that impair CD8+ T-cell and NK-cell cytotoxicity. The I3LUNG prospective multimodal cohort (Nature Medicine, 2025; n>3,500, 18 institutions, 10 countries) provides unprecedented evidence that CHIP-positive NSCLC patients receiving first-line chemo-immunotherapy have significantly worse progression-free and overall survival, independent of PD-L1 expression and TMB. These data establish CHIP as a clinically actionable, mechanism-grounded biomarker whose full predictive potential can only be captured through AI-integrated multimodal analysis.
1.3 The Need for Explainable AI in Clinical Oncology
The complexity of CHIP's immune interactions encompassing clonal genotype, purinergic metabolite dynamics, spatial immune architecture, radiomic tumor signatures, and longitudinal liquid biopsy trajectories, exceeds the capacity of conventional statistical models. Recent advances in explainable AI (XAI), including attention-based transformers, SHAP-value decomposition, and multimodal fusion architectures, provide the computational foundation for integrating these heterogeneous data streams in a model agnostic manner, while maintaining the clinical interpretability required for bedside deployment. However, no XAI framework has been developed or validated for CHIP-inclusive multimodal biomarker integration in oncology. CHIP-PULSE will fill this gap by combining the clinical and biological expertise of the Garassino Laboratory at the [PS1.1]University of Chicago Medicine with the exascale AI computing capabilities of Madduri’s team at Argonne National Laboratory whose work leveraging Aurora supercomputer— one of the world's most powerful supercomputers — to build, train, and validate the first purpose-built CHIP-aware AI platform for ICI response prediction.
2. Innovation
CHIP-PULSE is innovative on three dimensions that are collectively unprecedented in the field:
Biomedical innovation: This is the first systematic integration of CHIP clonal dynamics with purinergic biomarkers (eATP, adenosine, P2X7-positive EVs) and high-dimensional immune profiling as a unified predictive input for ICI response. The mechanistic grounding of the AI model in the eATP–P2X7–EV circuit provides a biologically interpretable substrate that goes beyond data-driven correlation.
Computational innovation: CHIP-PULSE will develop novel multimodal transformer architectures capable of processing temporally structured, missing-data-tolerant inputs spanning molecular assays, imaging features, and longitudinal liquid biopsy — trained at scale on Aurora's 21,000+ GPU nodes and 9 exaflops of sustained computing power, enabling model complexity and training regimes impossible on conventional infrastructure.[MR2.1]
Clinical deployment innovation: Trained models will be implemented within a clinical decision-support interface integrated with UChicago's EHR system and prospectively validated across I3LUNG's 18 international sites, establishing a generalizable framework for CHIP-aware precision immunotherapy that can be adapted to other tumor types.
3. Research Approach
3.1 Overview and Conceptual Framework
CHIP-PULSE is structured around three integrated Research Tasks (RTs), designed to progress from data integration infrastructure (RT1) through AI model development and validation (RT2) to prospective clinical deployment and continuous learning (RT3). All three RTs are conducted in parallel with continuous cross-team integration, coordinated through biweekly joint meetings between the UChicago clinical team and the Argonne AI team.
3.2 Research Task 1: Multimodal Data Integration and Feature Engineering (Year 1–2)
Objective: Construct a harmonized, analysis-ready multimodal dataset from the I3LUNG UChicago cohort and prospective extension (target n=450 advanced NSCLC patients receiving first-line chemo-immunotherapy) integrating the following data streams:
(a) CHIP profiling: whole-genome sequencing (80× depth) for CHIP variant calling (DNMT3A, TET2, ASXL1, JAK2, SF3B1, SRSF2, IDH1/2) with clonal fraction quantification and longitudinal tracking via serial cfDNA. (b) Purinergic biomarker panel: plasma eATP (bioluminescence assay), adenosine, soluble P2X7 receptor, P2X7-positive EV quantification (nanoparticle tracking analysis + flow cytometry), and inflammatory cytokine panel (IL-1β, IL-6, IL-18, TNF-α, IL-33; O-Link proximity extension). (c) High-dimensional immune phenotyping: mass cytometry (CyTOF; 42-parameter panel) and single-cell RNA-seq on PBMC and paired tumor-infiltrating lymphocytes at baseline, cycle 2, and disease evaluation. (d) Spatial multi-omics: CODEX spatial proteomics on FFPE tumor sections (45-marker panel) for characterization of the tumor immune microenvironment, myeloid polarization states, and spatial proximity of CHIP-expanded myeloid clones to T/NK cells. (e) Radiomics: extraction of >1,000 radiomic features from baseline and first-response CT scans using PyRadiomics, with standardization per IBSI guidelines. (f) Longitudinal ctDNA: serial liquid biopsy (Guardant360 platform) for tumor clonal dynamics monitoring. (g) Clinical and outcomes data: treatment regimen, response (RECIST 1.1), PFS, OS, immune-related adverse events.
Data harmonization [MR3.1][MR3.2]will follow FAIR principles with standardized ontologies (SNOMED-CT, LOINC, DICOM) and federated data governance protocols compliant with IRB requirements across I3LUNG sites. A federated learning architecture will enable model training across international sites without raw data transfer, using differential privacy guarantees.[PS4.1]
3.3 Research Task 2: Explainable AI Model Development and Validation (Year 2–3)
Objective: Develop, train, and validate a CHIP-aware explainable AI model for ICI response prediction and early treatment failure detection, leveraging Aurora exascale computing at Argonne National Laboratory.
Architecture: We will develop a multimodal hierarchical transformer (MHT) that processes each data modality through a modality-specific encoder (genomic transformer for CHIP dynamics, temporal LSTM for purinergic/ctDNA trajectories, vision transformer for radiomic features, graph neural network for spatial omics data) before cross-modal attention fusion at a shared latent space. This architecture accommodates temporally structured inputs, handles missing modalities through masked attention, and produces calibrated probabilistic predictions of ICI response (complete/partial response vs. stable/progressive disease at 12 weeks).
Explainability: Model interpretability will be achieved through (a) SHAP-value decomposition applied at two levels: feature-level SHAP to quantify individual predictor contributions for a given patient, and modality-level Shapley values that treat each encoder (genomic, temporal, radiomic, spatial omics) as a coalition member to quantify its marginal contribution to the fused prediction; (b) Integrated Gradients for attribution of prediction to specific CHIP genotype–purinergic feature combinations; (c) concept-based explanations (TCAV) linking learned latent features to biologically meaningful concepts (e.g., "myeloid immunosuppression score," "purinergic amplification index"); and (d) counterfactual explanation generation ("If this patient's eATP trajectory decreased by 40% at cycle 2, the predicted response probability would increase from 22% to 61%); and (e) modality-specific explainers matched to each modality’s encoder: GNNExplainer for spatial omics subgraph attribution, TimeSHAP for cycle-level trajectory attribution, and transformer relevance propagation for token- and patch-level attribution in the genomic and radiomic branches.
Training and validation: Primary training on the UChicago I3LUNG cohort (n=300[PS5.1][NT5.2]; using nested cross validation, with Aurora enabling hyperparameter search across >10,000 configurations, ensemble training of 100+ models, and uncertainty quantification via Monte Carlo dropout and Bayesian deep learning. External validation will be conducted across five independent I3LUNG international sites (n=150) in a pre-specified federated validation protocol. The primary endpoint is area under the ROC curve (AUC) for ICI response prediction, with pre-specified superiority threshold of AUC >0.80 and superiority vs. PD-L1 + TMB alone; calibration and per-site performance with 95% confidence intervals are secondary.
3.4 Research Task 3: Clinical Deployment, Prospective Validation, and Continuous Learning (Year 3–4)
Objective: Deploy the validated CHIP-PULSE model within the UChicago Epic EHR environment as a clinical decision-support tool, conduct prospective validation in a 100-patient observational cohort, and implement a continuous learning pipeline for model updating as new data accrue.
Clinical interface: The CHIP-PULSE dashboard will display individualized ICI response probability, SHAP-based feature+modality attribution, temporal biomarker trajectory plots, and comparative risk stratification (patient vs. I3LUNG matched cohort). The interface will be co-designed with oncologists, advanced practice providers, and patient advocates through a structured human-centered design process.
Prospective validation: 100 consecutive advanced NSCLC patients initiating first-line chemo-immunotherapy at UChicago will be enrolled. CHIP-PULSE predictions will be generated at baseline and cycle 2 but withheld from treating physicians (observational design). Primary endpoint: prospective AUC for ICI response at 12 weeks. Secondary endpoints: prediction of ICIs [PS6.1]risk, lead-time advantage vs. RECIST progression, and model calibration by CHIP genotype subgroup.
Federated Continuous [PS7.1][NT7.2][NT7.3]learning: A federated continuous learning pipeline will enable quarterly model updates as new patient data accumulate across I3LUNG sites, using a privacy-preserving gradient aggregation protocol. Drift detection algorithms will monitor for population shifts that require model recalibration, ensuring sustained performance as treatment protocols evolve.
4. Evaluation Plan
Progress will be evaluated against pre-specified quantitative milestones at 6-month intervals throughout the project period. Milestones include: (Year 1) harmonized multimodal dataset assembled for n≥200 patients with ≥4 data modalities per patient; (Year 2) baseline MHT model achieving AUC ≥0.75 in internal validation; (Year 3) external validation AUC ≥0.80 across five I3LUNG sites, superiority vs. PD-L1+TMB established; (Year 4) prospective cohort enrolled (n=100), model deployed in Epic, continuous learning pipeline operational. An independent External Advisory Board (EAB) comprising two clinical oncologists, two AI/ML scientists, and one bioethicist will convene annually to review progress, data quality, model performance, and ethical deployment considerations. Formal Go/No-Go decision points are built into the transition from RT1→RT2 (data quality threshold) and RT2→RT3 (validation AUC threshold).
5. Broader Impacts
CHIP-PULSE will generate broad scientific, clinical, educational, and societal impacts. Scientifically, it will establish a reproducible, open-source framework for CHIP-aware multimodal AI in oncology, with code, pre-trained models, and de-identified training data released via GitHub and the NIH Data Commons. Clinically, a validated CHIP-PULSE risk stratification tool will be immediately actionable in the >200,000 advanced NSCLC patients treated annually in the United States alone, enabling precision ICI selection and monitoring. Societally, the project will reduce unnecessary ICI exposure — with its associated toxicity burden and cost — in patients unlikely to benefit, while concentrating therapy in those most likely to respond. Educational impacts include training of three PhD students and two postdoctoral fellows in cross-disciplinary computational oncology, with dedicated mentorship for trainees from underrepresented groups through UChicago's MRSEC program. Annual open-access workshops on CHIP-aware AI in oncology will be held at ASCO and AACR, and course materials will be made freely available.
6. Timeline
Year 1: IRB amendments; I3LUNG data harmonization; biobank sample processing; CHIP WGS (n=200); multimodal feature engineering pipeline development; baseline model architecture design.
Year 2: Complete biomarker data collection (n=300); MHT model training on Aurora; hyperparameter optimization; internal validation; SHAP/explainability framework development; federated validation protocol finalization.
Year 3: External validation across 5 I3LUNG sites; Epic integration development; EHR interface co-design; prospective cohort enrollment initiation (n=100); first continuous learning pipeline deployment; EAB review.
Year 4: Prospective cohort completion and primary endpoint analysis; full continuous learning pipeline operational; model dissemination; open-source release; training program completion; final reporting.
7. Team and Resources
The CHIP-PULSE team combines world-leading expertise in clinical thoracic oncology, CHIP biology, AI/ML, and exascale computing:
PI — Marina Chiara Garassino, M.D. (UChicago Medicine): International leader in NSCLC clinical trials and biomarker research; PI of I3LUNG (Nature Medicine, 2025); co-inventor of CHIP-lung cancer interaction framework; 25% effort.
Co-PI [PS8.1][MR8.2]— [Ravi Madduri, Data Science and Learning Division] (Argonne National Laboratory): Expert in large-scale AI model training on Aurora exascale infrastructure; extensive experience in federated learning and biomedical AI; 25% effort.
Co-Investigator — [Bioinformatics/Genomics, UChicago]: CHIP variant calling, WGS analysis pipeline; 15% effort.
Co-Investigator — [Spatial Omics/Immunology, UChicago]: CyTOF, scRNA-seq, CODEX spatial proteomics; 15% effort.
Senior Personnel — [Clinical Trials Coordination, UChicago]: IRB, patient enrollment, biobank, prospective cohort; 10% effort.
Resources: UChicago Medicine provides access to the I3LUNG international cohort, UChicago Comprehensive Cancer Center clinical trial infrastructure, Epic EHR integration capabilities, and dedicated laboratory space. Argonne National Laboratory provides Aurora exascale computing allocation (estimated 50M GPU-hours), [PS9.1][NT9.2][MR9.3]the Argonne Leadership Computing Facility data management infrastructure, and federated learning platform expertise.
 
COLLABORATION PLAN
(Maximum 2 pages — Required Supplementary Document)
1. Team Composition and Roles
CHIP-PULSE integrates two principal institutions — the University of Chicago Medicine (UChicago) and Argonne National Laboratory (ANL) — in a deeply collaborative partnership that is scientifically essential, not merely administrative. Neither institution alone has the combination of clinical data, biomarker infrastructure, and exascale AI computing required to execute this project.
University of Chicago Medicine (Lead Institution): Clinical and translational leadership. Responsible for patient enrollment and biosampling; CHIP WGS and purinergic biomarker assays; spatial multi-omics; I3LUNG cohort data access and international site coordination; Epic EHR integration; IRB oversight; and clinical outcomes data management. The Garassino Laboratory provides the primary scientific direction, biomarker design, and clinical interpretation of model outputs.
Argonne National Laboratory (Subaward): Computational and AI leadership. Responsible for multimodal hierarchical transformer architecture development; Aurora exascale model training and hyperparameter optimization; explainability framework implementation (SHAP, Integrated Gradients, TCAV); federated learning infrastructure and differential privacy protocols; continuous learning pipeline; and model dissemination infrastructure. ANL provides primary AI/ML scientific direction and computing resource management.
2. Management and Coordination
The project will be managed through a Joint Steering Committee (JSC) co-chaired by the PI (Garassino, UChicago) and the AI Co-PI (ANL). The JSC will convene biweekly via video conference to coordinate data transfer, model development milestones, and cross-team integration. A shared project management platform (GitHub for code; REDCap for clinical data; shared Confluence wiki for documentation) will be maintained by a 0.5 FTE project coordinator at UChicago.
An External Advisory Board (EAB) of five independent experts (two clinical oncologists, two AI/ML scientists, one bioethicist) will convene annually to review scientific progress, data quality, model performance, ethical deployment considerations, and milestone achievement. EAB reports will be provided to NSF at each annual reporting period.
3. Cross-Disciplinary Integration Mechanisms
Scientific integration across institutions is ensured through four specific mechanisms: (1) Monthly joint laboratory meetings alternating between clinical and computational teams, ensuring mutual understanding of biological constraints on model design and computational constraints on biomarker data collection protocols. (2) Cross-trained personnel: one UChicago postdoc will spend Year 1–2 embedded at Argonne to develop deep familiarity with the AI pipeline; one ANL postdoc will spend Year 2–3 embedded at UChicago to develop biomedical domain knowledge. (3) Shared milestone tracking: all Go/No-Go milestones require sign-off by both institution leads before progression, preventing siloed development. (4) Joint authorship and publication policy: all primary manuscripts will require minimum one co-author from each institution.
4. Budget Line Items Supporting Collaboration
The budget directly funds collaboration through: UChicago–ANL data transfer infrastructure and secure computing environment (~$45K Year 1); cross-embedded postdoc travel and temporary housing support (~$20K/year); biannual in-person joint team meetings alternating between Chicago and Lemont, IL (~$15K/year); EAB annual meeting and honoraria (~$10K/year); and dedicated project coordination (0.5 FTE at UChicago, ~$40K/year including fringe).
 
